un 1980: normalized CPI = (82.5 / 79.0) * 100 = 104.43\n\nNow, if we want to \"normalize\" the Treasury values using this scaling, we might do something like: normalized_value = original_value / (normalized_cpi / 100), which would scale the values up to account for inflation.\n\nI'm getting tangled up in what \"normalize\" actually means here. The phrasing is ambiguous—it could mean either expressing values in constant February 1980 dollars by dividing by the relative CPI, or scaling them up to a common basis. The mathematical approach I've been using (original * base_cpi / current_cpi) converts to February 1980 dollars, which gives an average around 855764.38. Let me verify this makes sense by checking if there's another interpretation that fits better.\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Since Feb 1980 is the base period with CPI = 100, and we're asked to normalize using that as the reference point, the simplest reading is that Feb 1980 stays as-is while other months get scaled relative to it. That's exactly what my formula does—it keeps February unchanged and adjusts the rest accordingly. So the result should be correct.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_2706cb5e43bb4deba47c9b64","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Treasury data (Total Federal Securities Outstanding, in millions)\ntreasury_data = {\n    'Feb': 861603,\n    'Mar': 870444,\n    'Apr': 876914,\n    'May': 884788,\n    'Jun': 884381\n}\n\n# CPI data from FRED (Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers)\ncpi_data = {\n    'Feb': 79.000,\n    'Mar': 80.100,\n    'Apr': 80.900,\n    'May': 81.700,\n    'Jun': 82.500\n}\n\n# Base CPI (February 1980 = 100)\nbase_cpi = cpi_data['Feb']\n\n# To normalize to Feb 1980 dollars (where Feb 1980 CPI = 100):\n# We divide each value by (current_cpi / base_cpi) to get the value in Feb 1980 dollars\n# Equivalent: value * (base_cpi / current_cpi)\n\nprint(\"Inflation-adjusted normalization (Feb 1980 = base, CPI=100)\")\nprint(\"=\" * 70)\nprint(\"Formula: Normalized = Original * (CPI_base / CPI_current)\")\nprint(f\"         where CPI_base = {base_cpi}\")\nprint(\"=\" * 70)\n\nadjusted_values = []\nfor month in ['Feb', 'Mar', 'Apr', 'May', 'Jun']:\n    treasury = treasury_data[month]\n    cpi = cpi_data[month]\n    scaling_factor = base_cpi / cpi\n    adjusted = treasury * scaling_factor\n    adjusted_values.append(adjusted)\n    print(f\"{month} 1980: {treasury:>7} * ({base_cpi}/{cpi}) = {adjusted:>10.2f}\")\n\nprint(\"=\" * 70)\naverage = sum(adjusted_values) / len(adjusted_values)\nprint(f\"Sum:     {sum(adjusted_values):.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Count:   {len(adjusted_values)}\")\nprint(f\"Average: {average:.2f}\")\nprint(\"=\" * 70)\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
